Project context — Postudio cross-lingual dubbing pipeline
What this is
This is the first version of an AI dubbing pipeline being built at Postudio, a cloud post-production company in India. We're building it to take Indian-language source content (Tamil, Hindi, eventually other Indic languages) and produce broadcast-quality English dubs with voice identity, emotional fidelity, and lip sync preserved.
The pipeline will eventually power a commercial product sold to Indian media companies (broadcasters, streaming platforms, microdrama producers, content studios) who currently pay for manual dubbing or use ElevenLabs Dubbing Studio with poor results on Indic languages.
This evaluation harness is the v0 architecture validation. Once we lock the architecture, we'll build production code on top.
Strategic positioning
We are NOT building a generic dubbing tool to compete with ElevenLabs globally. We are building the best dubbing tool specifically for Indian-language content going to global English audiences (and eventually other target languages).
Our moats vs. ElevenLabs:

Editor-corrected diarization data accumulated through Postudio's existing platform
1500+ hours of broadcast-grade audio data we can use for training
Distribution through Postudio's existing customer relationships
Workflow integration inside Postudio's editor platform
Fine-tuning on Indic content over time
Pronunciation dictionary for Indic names/terms built from QC corrections

ElevenLabs has technology lead on TTS itself. We don't compete with that — we use ElevenLabs v3 TTS API as a component. Our pipeline wraps it with components ElevenLabs does poorly: Indic diarization, duration-aware translation, voice identity preservation, prosody handling, workflow integration.
Critical product requirements — DO NOT compromise on these
Voice identity preservation is non-negotiable
When a Tamil actor speaks in the source video, the English output must sound like THE SAME PERSON speaking English — not a generic English voice replacing them. This is the entire point of dubbing. Without voice identity preservation, we're just generating English audio that happens to be timed to a video, which is not a product anyone pays for.
This means:

We MUST use voice cloning, not library voices
We MUST clone the source actor's voice from source audio segments
We MUST use the cloned voice for all generations of that character

Concretely: for ElevenLabs v3 TTS, this means creating an Instant Voice Clone (IVC) from a clean sample of each character's source audio, then generating English dialogue using that IVC as the voice.
DO NOT default to library voices "for simplicity" or "for testing." Library voices are useful only for sanity-checking that the TTS API call works at all. Once we know the TTS pipeline runs, switch immediately to cloned voices. If you're unsure, ask before defaulting to library voices.
American English accent is non-negotiable
Output must be American English regardless of source language. Indian English with Tamil/Hindi accent is NOT acceptable for our customer base — they're producing content for global English-speaking audiences who expect American or neutral international English.
This creates a tension with voice cloning: cloning a Tamil actor's voice will leak Tamil accent into the English output. We will solve this with one of:

ElevenLabs v3 Audio Tags steering toward American accent
Post-processing accent conversion (Krisp, Tomato.ai, Respeecher) if accent leak is too strong
STS pass with American voice as target (handled separately, not in this pipeline today)

For TODAY's testing, accept some accent leak as a known issue. Document segments where accent leak is severe so we can prioritize accent conversion as a follow-up component.
Duration fitting matters more than translation perfection
When translating source dialogue to English, the output duration MUST fit the source segment duration. A 4-second Tamil segment should produce a ~4-second English TTS output. If the translation produces 6 seconds of English, we cannot use it — speeding up to fit the gap creates the unnatural rushed delivery that ruins ElevenLabs Dubbing Studio's output.
This means translation must be duration-aware from the start, not translated freely and stretched to fit. The LLM call for translation must include target duration as a hard constraint and be told to be concise to fit.
Emotion preservation matters
Source actors deliver lines with emotion — anger, sadness, excitement, whispers. The English output must preserve that emotional delivery, not produce flat neutral readings. ElevenLabs v3 supports this via Audio Tags ([angry], [excited], [whispers], [shouts] etc.) — these tags must be inserted into translated text based on source emotion analysis.
Source emotion analysis options: prosodic feature extraction (pitch contour, energy, speaking rate via librosa) plus source text context, fed to LLM with instruction to inject appropriate Audio Tags.
What customers will and won't accept
Customers (Indian media companies) will accept:

Some accent leak from source language
Library voices ONLY if explicitly chosen for non-character roles (narrator, off-screen voice)
Translation that adapts cultural references appropriately rather than literal translation
Songs/music left in source language with subtitles (industry convention)

Customers will NOT accept:

Generic English voices replacing recognizable actor voices
Robotic or monotone delivery that lacks emotion
Word-for-word literal translation
Sped-up or stretched audio that sounds unnatural
Audio that's clearly AI-generated to a casual listener

The bar is "indistinguishable from human dubbing for casual viewers" — not "perfect" but "good enough that the average viewer doesn't notice it's AI."
Technical architecture decisions already made
These are decided. Don't propose alternatives:

TTS: ElevenLabs v3 plain TTS API (not Studio API, not Dubbing API)
Voice cloning: ElevenLabs IVC (Instant Voice Clone), one clone per character per episode
Translation: Gemini API with engineered prompt for duration awareness + emotion tagging
Source: Tamil today, Hindi tomorrow, language-agnostic architecture
Output: American English, broadcast-quality (44.1kHz)

These are open and being evaluated:

Stem separation: testing OSS (BS-RoFormer, Mel-RoFormer, HTDemucs) vs commercial (AudioShake, ElevenLabs Audio Isolation)
Diarization: testing OSS (Pyannote 3.1) vs commercial (AssemblyAI, Speechmatics, Deepgram). Note: we have editor-corrected diarization data from Postudio platform that may serve as ground truth or replace fresh diarization entirely
ASR: testing OSS (IndicWhisper) vs commercial (Gemini, Deepgram, Scribe v2)
Lip sync: testing OSS (LatentSync, MuseTalk) vs commercial (Sync.so)

Reasoning style for this project
When making decisions or proposing alternatives, default to:

"Use the cloned voice" over "use a library voice for simplicity"
"Preserve emotion via tags" over "neutral delivery is fine for testing"
"Fit duration via translation constraint" over "speed up audio to fit"
"Test commercial APIs as a benchmark" over "OSS is always better"
"Keep architecture language-agnostic" over "hardcode for Tamil"

When in doubt about a product decision, ASK. Defaulting to "simpler for testing" often defaults away from the product requirements and creates work to undo later. The right test of a dubbing pipeline is one that produces dubbed output a customer would accept, not one that just runs end-to-end with placeholder components.
Out of scope today

STS (speech-to-speech voice conversion) — handled separately, not in this pipeline
Production deployment, error handling, retry logic — this is an evaluation harness
Multi-episode batch processing — single video for now
Cost optimization — quality first, cost second
Songs/music handling — known limitation, defer
Whispered or shouted extreme emotion — known hard cases, defer
Children's voices, elderly voices — known hard cases, defer
Crowd scenes with overlapping voices — known hard cases, defer

Success criteria for today
A pipeline that:

Takes dub_original.mp4 (Tamil source) as input
Produces a dubbed English video at results/final_output.mp4
Where each character's English voice sounds like the original character (cloned voice, not library voice)
Where emotion is preserved via Audio Tags
Where audio fits source segment durations (no severe speedup/stretching)
Where lip sync is at least attempted (LSE-D measured even if not optimal)
Where each component has been tested across multiple alternatives with comparison data
Where switching to Hindi tomorrow requires only changing config

If any of these is missing, the pipeline is not done.